# Add server actions with Datastar

Rocci uses Datastar as a transport for server-rendered updates. The browser
declares an action, the Roc handler changes state, and the response either
morphs a fragment in **this tab** or, with `@live`, lets a generated stream
morph a shared region in every open tab.

Two shipped patterns:

| Pattern | Author writes | What updates |
| --- | --- | --- |
| One-shot patch | `@on:post` returning Html | The acting tab, via one `datastar-patch-elements` |
| Live CQRS | `@live` returning Html; commands marked `json` | Every tab on `GET /sse`; POSTs return 204 (Datastar) or JSON (`curl`) |

The first-app counter (`examples/rocci/standalone/counter`) stays one-shot. Two
browsers that should stay in sync use `examples/rocci/standalone/live-counter`
or the hybrid island `examples/rocdown/counter`. Hybrid hosting notes are in
[Publish a hybrid Rocdown site](/docs/guides/hybrid-sites/).

## Declare application state

```rocci
import pf.Sqlite

@context { db : Sqlite.Db }

@init {
    db = Sqlite.open!(Sqlite.default_config(Path.utf8("./counter.db")))?
    { db: db }
}
```

`@context` defines the state record used by generated handlers. `@init` creates
that state once when the standalone app starts.

## Serve the document

```rocci
@on:get("/") = |{ db }| {
    count = read_count!(db)?
    counterPage({ count })
}
```

GET handlers return complete HTML documents. Rocci generates a basic-webserver
dispatcher around the authored function. Declare `request` as a second
parameter when the handler reads `Server.Request`; omit it otherwise.

## Return a targeted patch

```rocci
@on:post("/actions/counter/increment") = |{ db }| {
    count = increment_count!(db)?
    counterCard({ count })
}

@component CounterCard = |{ count }|
    <section id="counter">
        <p>Shared count</p>
        <output>{count.to_str()}</output>
    </section>
```

Non-GET handlers without a respond ident return one-shot
`datastar-patch-elements` events. The stable `id` lets Datastar target the
existing fragment. Only the tab that posted updates.

## Share a live region

`@live` is the opt-in for a generated shared stream. One per module, same
parameter rules as `@on`. Rocci emits `GET /sse` (poll + patch) and, when the
document `<body>` has no `data-init`, injects
`data-init=@get("/sse", [OpenWhenHidden(True)])`.

```rocci
@live = |{ db }| {
    count = read_count!(db)?
    counterCard({ count })
}

@on:post("/actions/counter/increment") json = |{ db }| {
    count = increment_count!(db)?
    "{\"count\":${count.to_str()}}"
}
```

Mark mutating commands `json`. When the request has `Datastar-Request: true`,
the dispatcher answers **204** (no morph). Otherwise it answers **200**
`application/json` with the handler `Str`. Do not return `counterCard` from a
`json` POST: that would patch `#counter` from both the command and the stream.
JSON `{ "count": N }` patches Datastar **signals**, not the DOM; bind the
visible count in HTML from the server, not `$count`.

`curl -X POST` without `Datastar-Request` prints JSON, not
`datastar-patch-elements`.

## Log from a handler

`@on` and `@init` are effectful Roc. Import `pf.Stderr` and write lines with
`Stderr.line!`. Under `rocci run`, those lines show on the CLI and in the
preview Dev Console (`source: runtime`). Do not put prints in `@component`
bodies; keep components pure. Match away `StderrErr` instead of using `?` when
the handler already propagates other error types (for example SQLite):

```rocci
import pf.Stderr

@on:post("/actions/counter/increment") = |{ db }| {
    count = increment_count!(db)?
    match Stderr.line!("counter increment count=${count.to_str()}") {
        Ok({}) => {}
        Err(_) => {}
    }
    counterCard({ count })
}
```

`Stdout.line!` reaches the terminal only. Prefer stderr when you want the
same line in Console.

## Trigger the action

```rocci
<button data-on:click=@post("/actions/counter/increment")>
    Increment
</button>
```

The `@post` attribute form is Rocci syntax. Rocci injects the Datastar import
and produces the client expression. Its argument is Roc and therefore uses
double-quoted Roc strings.

> A quoted value such as `data-on:click="@post('/actions/x')"` stays an opaque
> Datastar expression. Use the unquoted Rocci action form when the URL depends
> on Roc values or when you want compiler help.

## Choose route conventions

| Response | Suggested route | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HTML document | Resource path | `GET /todos` |
| HTML or SSE patch | `/actions/...` | `POST /actions/todos/add` |
| JSON command (`json`) | `/actions/...` | `POST /actions/counter/increment` → 204 or `{"count":N}` |
| Long-lived SSE | `/sse` | Generated from `@live`; custom unfold stays in authored `main.roc` |

## Run the examples

```sh
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- run examples/rocci/standalone/counter/Counter.rocci
cargo run -q -p rocci-cli -- run examples/rocci/standalone/live-counter/LiveCounter.rocci
```

In the first-app counter, increment in two windows: SQLite is shared, but the
second tab stays stale until you click or refresh. In live-counter, both
windows morph `#counter` from the stream. See
[the rendering model](/docs/concepts/rendering-model/) for the boundary between
documents, patches, JSON, and streams.

The hybrid site form of this card (`examples/rocdown/counter`) is live: the
document is CDN HTML (snapshot `0`), `@live` plus `json` commands run on
`rocdown serve-islands`, and the first stream event replaces the snapshot.
